[Music]  the smp has been in power in hollywood  for 15 years now in this week's magazine  james heal the spectators diary editor  and michael simmons our data journalist  write that nicholas sturgeon has created  one of the most opaque and centralized  governments in europe  to discuss james joins me now alongside  jackie bailey deputy leader of scottish  labour  james and jackie thanks for joining me  on spectator tv  james you along with our colleague  michael simmons write this week about  the quote poor planning willful waste  and absence of accountability end quote  of the scottish national party over the  past 15 years in power  what in your view has gone so badly  wrong  okay i think a number of things have  gone wrong up in scotland um i think the  danger is that secret government tends  to lead to bad government with a lack of  transparency accountability mechanisms  for some of the failures so those  failures are repeated and exacerbated  and the focal point for this story was  looking at the um ferguson shipyard and  what's gone wrong there and now this is  sort of eight years of delay uh  misspending and the original budget for  these two original vessels has gone from  97 million to 240 million  and so what the real issue is that not  things that have gone wrong but that  these failures have been exacerbated by  a failure to learn from mistakes and the  danger you have with the s p i think is  a party that's been in power 15 years  now and a tendency to think of itself as  the state and the danger is that any  criticism is seen as somehow treason  and therefore you do not learn from  what's gone wrong previously and um it  just means a failure to learn and that's  why scotland i think is suffering in its  public sector outcomes  jackie is james being unfair i mean  things go wrong all the time in the  public sector doesn't necessarily mean  that something particularly sinister is  going on  no i think he's perhaps being overly  generous if i could put it that way you  know i've been in the scottish  parliament since 1999 the very beginning  and i have never seen a more  centralizing more secretive government  than the one that's currently in power  um and james is right they simply do not  accept they make mistakes or indeed  learn from them  and what we're witnessing now is very  much a  centralizing command and control  structure that's controlled by nicolas  sturgeon at the very top  dissent is just not allowed debate is  stifled  and the committees of the parliament do  not have teeth they don't seem to be  able to challenge the first minister  because of course the snp majority votes  as one you know we don't have  independent members on committees they  are very much creatures of their own  party if you take the ferguson shipyard  contract as an example but it is  excessively over budget  documents are missing that would tell us  what actually happened  and it seems to be  that key points in the contract were  based on  snp conference or the referendum and if  you look at the timeline there's  certainly in my view  not just serial incompetence which is  what i used to think it was but actually  an abuse of power and it is a  disgraceful scandal and  nobody is shedding light on it and we  need to understand the truth of what  went on james can you talk a bit about  nicholas sturgeon's office which you and  michael mentioned in the piece how it  works and why you think it differs from  number 10 i mean is it really any more  secretive than what's happening in  whitehall  well i think in whitehall you certainly  have a degree to which is based on a  balance of power you know you have the  treasury you have the foreign office you  have a number of different whitehall  departments competing and some might say  that kind of competition does produce at  least a degree of accountability whereby  you know you have a number of different  ministers you can hold that prime  minister to account you know the prime  minister here for instance london  doesn't even have a department of his  own whereas in scotland if you talk to  civil servants people involved in the  government there i mean nicola sturgeon  sort of looms like a zeppelin over the  whole thing  very even sort of minor decisions get  signed off by the first minister which  is why some people find it hard to  believe that in the ferguson shipyard  she wasn't involved in signing off any  of it and that's why i've seen a lot of  the briefings going on and a lot of the  kind of spin that's happened um because  of what's happened and what i think is  most important kate is that the scottish  government was set up and the scottish  apartment was set up in 1999 and there  was a real hope and expectation about it  here was a chance for scotland and  hollywood to do much better than  westminster and the sad thing is that  nicola sturgeon a woman who was elected  in 2014 as first minister to be the most  quote accessible first minister ever  hasn't lived up to that expectation and  promise and that kind of self-renewal  that was hoped for with the s p  when she was named first minister after  alex salmon left hasn't really taken  place and so i think the difference  between the aspiration and reality or  the rhetoric of reality is what is most  damning about what's going on in  scotland jackie james writes about  sturgeon's latest idea as part of a  pandemic law the scottish government  would be able to modify or amend any act  of parliament without a vote  what do you make of those plans are they  a non-starter in hollywood  i mean frankly i find them quite  extraordinary um it is astonishing  what's being described as sweeping henry  viii powers  that the scottish government is seeking  will enable them without scrutiny  without approval of the scottish  parliament to simply change existing  primary legislation in whatever way they  wish now i think that frankly is just a  power grab too far um if you look at the  consultation civic scotland is opposed  to this in fact i think 80 if not 90  percent of the respondents said that  this just went too far  and was not appropriate unfortunately  the snp now have a majority in the  parliament alongside their partners the  greens and so this will go through  unless we can make sufficient noise to  stop it from happening but it is quite  extraordinary that they now disregard  the parliament completely even during  the pandemic whilst it is appropriate to  give people emergency powers and the  parliament was able to do that quite  quickly and the reality is when it comes  to lane regulations they lay them and  only see parliamentary approval after  the event that's not a smart way of  doing it if you want to take everybody  with you but as you say jackie the snp  has their majority you can make a lot of  noise but realistically is there much  more that you could do to stop them  well i think and what we've sought to do  is when you find that the government  isn't listening then actually making  common cause with civic scotland with  the experts and the professionals  whether it's in academia or in business  actually bears fruit because if the  entire country is saying this is not the  way to do things  then i do think any government including  nicolas sturgeon's government really  need to sit up and listen um but but i  live in hope that that might happen but  at the moment what we're seeing is spin  and assertion over evidence and scrutiny  and it's really depressing because  devolution could be so much better than  this james you and michael write that  there are structural problems in the  scottish parliament too you write about  the warnings that snps were given during  the alex salmon trial for example  yes i mean i think that jackie touched  on an important point which is that you  know as long as you can the the one big  thing with the scottish electoral system  was that no one people thought that no  one ever get a majority but once you've  got that effectively there aren't many  checks or balances you know compared to  westminster where you've got for  instance the house of lords we've got a  much more dynamic political culture in  terms of you know back benches you know  conservative mps holding a conservative  government to account all the same with  labor uh the dangers you have those kind  of things um with the s p up in scotland  um and i think that the danger is that  with some of the alexander stuff what  went on was frankly um a very dangerous  mix of a separation of powers being  blurred um and  frankly the mechanism in scotland was  shown to be lacking and the question is  that now we have as jackie says an s p  green uh pro-independence governments  are just about propped up  will we see any of those failures being  addressed and typically what's happened  in scotland over the last 15 years is  that failures or institutions which are  shown to be wanting have not been  rectified have not had any kind of sort  of minor political constitutional reform  and so it's the institutions that have  been found wanting and the danger is  that there seems to be no impetus from  the governing parties to rectify that  any time soon  james you you say in your piece that the  boundaries between the party and the  state up in scotland have in some cases  become indistinguishable you mentioned  the shipyard are there other cases  yes certainly is and i think that a  number of civil servants  up in scotland um you know most recently  leslie evans who was the permanent  secretary of the scottish government  until she recently left um earlier this  year um have close connections to um the  party and the danger is of course is  that if you have a nationalist party in  government the tendency is to see that  party as the party of government and the  only legitimate voice in scotland and i  think that jackie and a number of other  sort of politicians take a different  view on that have had those voices  attempting to be shut down and i don't  think that's particularly healthy or  productive for scott so at the end that  they  have to live with a result of policies  that politicians put through  jackie last question to you you said in  february last year during the fallout  between nicola sturgeon and alex salmond  that quote there's something rotten at  the core of the smp and it is poisoning  our democratic institutions end quote  now as somebody who works inside  scotland's political system every day  have you noticed any changes have things  become better since last february  no in fact they've become worse  i think the scottish government is less  accountable more centralizing much more  secretive you know i i lived through the  salmon inquiry and i have to say the  level of obstruction from the scottish  government was absolutely legendary you  know we were given um papers only after  we sought them  doggedly for months and months what we  were given was by and large redacted i  mean i recall a document of 22 pages  where 20 pages were actually snow white  because everything had been redacted  and they just simply refused to hand  over information i mean they didn't just  do it to a parliamentary committee they  did it in the courts as well and judges  had to order the government to hand over  information but you know one example i  would give you just recently  is where the permanent secretary who is  retiring was asked to appear before the  finance and public administration  committee of the parliament you would  think it was an opportunity for her to  talk about her achievements she refused  to attend a serving senior civil servant  simply refused to attend the committee  um and she wasn't held to account by the  scottish government um and therefore she  was clearly too busy just  preparing for her retirement i find that  scandalous that the highest civil  servant in scotland is not instructed by  ministers to attend a parliamentary  committee  jackie and james thanks for joining  [Music]  you

